# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = '2 Kings'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'history'
SUBTITLE = 'Elijah Taken Up, the Fall of Israel and Judah, and the Exile to Babylon'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', '2 Kings follows Elijah’s going up by a whirlwind and Elisha’s ministry, then the fall of the northern kingdom to Assyria and the fall of Jerusalem to Babylon. It gives the reasons the book itself states, the prayer of Hezekiah, the finding of the book of the law under Josiah, and the carrying away of Judah, with a closing note on Jehoiachin (2 Kgs. 2:11; 17:7, 18, 23; 19:15; 22:8; 25:1, 7).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where Israel and Judah answer to the LORD’s covenant, are carried away from the land, and the house of David is still remembered (2 Kgs. 13:23; 17:23; 19:34).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of 2 Kings', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Elijah and Elisha (Chs. 1–8)'), ('p', 'Elijah goes up by a whirlwind, Naaman confesses there is no God but in Israel, and Elisha says, “they that be with us are more than they that be with them” (2 Kgs. 2:11; 5:15; 6:16).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Fall of Israel (Chs. 9–17)'), ('p', 'The LORD is gracious for His covenant with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, yet Israel sins, is removed out of His sight and carried to Assyria (2 Kgs. 13:23; 17:7, 14, 18, 23).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Hezekiah and Josiah (Chs. 18–23)'), ('p', 'Hezekiah trusts the LORD and prays, the LORD defends the city, and Josiah turns to the LORD after the book of the law is found (2 Kgs. 18:5; 19:15, 19, 34; 20:6; 22:8, 19; 23:25).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Fall of Judah (Chs. 24–25)'), ('p', 'Nebuchadnezzar comes against Jerusalem, Zedekiah is bound and carried to Babylon, and the book closes with Jehoiachin’s allowance (2 Kgs. 25:1, 7, 30).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in 2 Kings', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>Why Israel Fell</b> — “The children of Israel had sinned against the LORD their God, which had brought them up out of the land of Egypt” (2 Kgs. 17:7).', '<b>Removed as the Prophets Said</b> — “Until the LORD removed Israel out of his sight, as he had said by all his servants the prophets” (2 Kgs. 17:23).', '<b>The Covenant Remembered</b> — “The LORD was gracious unto them… because of his covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob” (2 Kgs. 13:23).', '<b>A Remnant</b> — “Out of Jerusalem shall go forth a remnant, and they that escape out of mount Zion” (2 Kgs. 19:31).', '<b>For David’s Sake</b> — “I will defend this city, to save it, for mine own sake, and for my servant David’s sake” (2 Kgs. 19:34).', '<b>The Book of the Law Found</b> — “I have found the book of the law in the house of the LORD” (2 Kgs. 22:8).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in 2 Kings', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how 2 Kings points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Remnant and David</b> — The LORD defends Jerusalem for David’s sake and speaks of a remnant going out of Zion (2 Kgs. 19:31, 34), within Israel’s prophetic hope.', '<b>The Prophet Taken Up</b> — Elijah goes up by a whirlwind into heaven (2 Kgs. 2:11); the book reports this as written.', '<b>Exile and Hope</b> — The book closes on Jehoiachin’s continual allowance (2 Kgs. 25:30), reported as written.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in 2 Kings', [('p', 'This section helps you see how 2 Kings speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. 2 Kings operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read 2 Kings in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', '2 Kings reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['covenant with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob (2 Kgs. 13:23)', 'removal from the land for sin (2 Kgs. 17:18, 23)', 'the remnant out of Jerusalem (2 Kgs. 19:31)', 'the house of David remembered (2 Kgs. 19:34; 20:6)', 'the book of the law (2 Kgs. 22:8)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in 2 Kings that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how 2 Kings contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['Israel Sinned — “the children of Israel had sinned against the LORD their God.” (2 Kgs. 17:7).', 'Removed Out of His Sight — “the LORD was very angry with Israel, and removed them out of his sight.” (2 Kgs. 17:18).', 'As the Prophets Said — “as he had said by all his servants the prophets.” (2 Kgs. 17:23).', 'The Covenant With the Fathers — “because of his covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.” (2 Kgs. 13:23).', 'The Remnant — “out of Jerusalem shall go forth a remnant.” (2 Kgs. 19:31).', 'For David’s Sake — “for my servant David’s sake.” (2 Kgs. 19:34).', 'The Book of the Law — “I have found the book of the law in the house of the LORD.” (2 Kgs. 22:8).', 'Josiah Turned — “that turned to the LORD with all his heart, and with all his soul, and with all his might.” (2 Kgs. 23:25).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What 2 Kings Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in 2 Kings and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what 2 Kings teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in 2 Kings', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Israel Sinned', '“the children of Israel had sinned against the LORD their God.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Kgs. 17:7'], ['Removed Out of His Sight', '“the LORD was very angry with Israel, and removed them out of his sight.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Kgs. 17:18'], ['As the Prophets Said', '“as he had said by all his servants the prophets.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Kgs. 17:23'], ['The Covenant With the Fathers', '“because of his covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Kgs. 13:23'], ['The Remnant', '“out of Jerusalem shall go forth a remnant.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Kgs. 19:31'], ['For David’s Sake', '“for my servant David’s sake.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Kgs. 19:34'], ['The Book of the Law', '“I have found the book of the law in the house of the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Kgs. 22:8'], ['Josiah Turned', '“that turned to the LORD with all his heart, and with all his soul, and with all his might.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '2 Kgs. 23:25']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in 2 Kings that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what 2 Kings teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['No God Like Him', '“thou art the God, even thou alone, of all the kingdoms of the earth; thou hast made heaven and earth.”', '2 Kgs. 19:15'], ['That All May Know', '“that all the kingdoms of the earth may know that thou art the LORD God, even thou only.”', '2 Kgs. 19:19'], ['The LORD Hears', '“I have heard thy prayer, I have seen thy tears.”', '2 Kgs. 20:5'], ['A Humble Heart Heard', '“thou hast humbled thyself before the LORD… I also have heard thee, saith the LORD.”', '2 Kgs. 22:19'], ['Those With Us Are More', '“they that be with us are more than they that be with them.”', '2 Kgs. 6:16']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of 2 Kings without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to 2 Kings in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Pray as Hezekiah did</b> — To the God who made heaven and earth (2 Kgs. 19:15).', '<b>Desire that all know the LORD</b> (2 Kgs. 19:19).', '<b>Trust that He hears</b> (2 Kgs. 20:5).', '<b>Humble yourself before Him</b> (2 Kgs. 22:19).', '<b>Take courage</b> — Those with us are more (2 Kgs. 6:16).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for 2 Kings', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach 2 Kings in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Elijah and Elisha (Chs. 1–8) — The prophets. <i>(2 Kgs. 2:11; 5:15; 6:16)</i>', 'The Fall of Israel (Chs. 9–17) — Removed as the prophets said. <i>(2 Kgs. 13:23; 17:7, 18, 23)</i>', 'Hezekiah and Josiah (Chs. 18–23) — Prayer and the book of the law. <i>(2 Kgs. 19:15, 31, 34; 22:8; 23:25)</i>', 'The Fall of Judah (Chs. 24–25) — Exile to Babylon. <i>(2 Kgs. 25:1, 7, 30)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read 2 Kings, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward humility, prayer, and trust without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching 2 Kings simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, 2 Kings stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
